WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:07.801
A set of double doors opened at the rear of the dining room, and Captain Nemo led me into the next room. It was a library.

2
00:00:07.802 --> 00:00:13.229
Tall, black bookcases lined the walls, and they held a huge number of books.

3
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:18.031
At the base of the bookcases were comfortable-looking couches covered in brown leather.

4
00:00:18.032 --> 00:00:23.689
In the center of the room stood an immense table littered with pamphlets and old newspapers.

5
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:28.943
Captain Nemo stretched out on a couch while I continued to admire his library.

6
00:00:28.944 --> 00:00:38.022
"This library belongs in a fine European palace," I said. "I'm absolutely astounded when I consider that it's at the bottom of the sea."

7
00:00:38.023 --> 00:00:45.022
"Where could one find greater solitude or silence than under the sea?" replied the captain.

8
00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:48.768
"Was your study at the museum as perfectly quiet as this?"

9
00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:53.435
"No, sir. You must have six or seven thousand books here."

10
00:00:53.436 --> 00:00:57.181
"Actually twelve thousand," said Captain Nemo.

11
00:00:57.182 --> 00:01:02.101
"I finished with the world on the day the Nautilus plunged beneath the waves for the first time.

12
00:01:02.102 --> 00:01:08.423
On that day I bought my last books and newspapers, and I like to think that people have written nothing more since then.

13
00:01:08.424 --> 00:01:11.339
Please feel free to make use of this library."

14
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.577
I began to examine the volumes on his shelves.

15
00:01:14.578 --> 00:01:23.665
With books in every language, his collection included works on science, ethics, and literature but nothing on politics or economics.

16
00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:27.808
By far the greatest number of books was about science.

17
00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:33.562
I saw a fairly new astronomy book that I knew had been published in 1864.

18
00:01:33.563 --> 00:01:39.483
From that, I concluded that the captain had been living underwater for at most three years.

19
00:01:39.484 --> 00:01:44.879
"Come, let's continue our tour," said the captain, rising from the couch.

20
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:51.625
A door opened in the wall opposite where we had entered the room, and I followed Captain Nemo into a spacious room.

21
00:01:51.626 --> 00:01:59.546
The splendid lighting revealed that this room was a large gallery filled with treasures from the natural and artistic worlds.The

22
00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:06.792
walls were adorned with more than thirty paintings by some of the most famous artists of the past and present. The

23
00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:13.188
old masters were represented by Raphael, da Vinci, Rubens, and many others. In

24
00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:18.117
the corners of the room stood some admirable statues in marble and bronze. "Sir,"

25
00:02:18.118 --> 00:02:25.188
I said, "without seeking to know who you are, I recognize that you are an artist." "No,

26
00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:33.680
merely a collector," said Captain Nemo. "I used to love to collect these beautiful works created by the hands of man. I

27
00:02:33.681 --> 00:02:39.180
greedily sought them out, and I've been able to bring together some objects of great value. But

28
00:02:39.181 --> 00:02:44.267
now they are my last reminders of a world that is dead to me." He

29
00:02:44.268 --> 00:02:50.585
seemed to go into a daydream and forget that I was there, so I took the opportunity to study the rest of the room. The

30
00:02:50.586 --> 00:02:58.077
next thing I noticed was a large organ upon which were strewn works by Mozart, Beethoven, and other great musicians. In

31
00:02:58.078 --> 00:03:06.910
the center of the room was a fountain with a single stream of water that rose into the air. The base was made from a giant clamshell. Around

32
00:03:06.911 --> 00:03:14.894
this fountain stood elegant glass cases filled with wonders from the sea. You can imagine my interest in those. One

33
00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:23.561
case was filled with coral, another with sponges. Yet another contained starfish, sea urchins, and sea cucumbers. Anyone

34
00:03:23.562 --> 00:03:31.457
interested in shells would have fainted at the sight of cases filled with so many rare and expensive specimens from around the globe. Another

35
00:03:31.458 --> 00:03:44.291
case was devoted solely to pearls: pink pearls, green pearls, yellow, blue, and black pearls, the unusual production of diverse oysters from every ocean. Some of

36
00:03:44.292 --> 00:03:47.712
these pearls were larger than birds' eggs! It was

37
00:03:47.713 --> 00:03:51.378
impossible to estimate the value of this collection. Captain Nemo

38
00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:56.616
must have spent millions to acquire these various natural specimens. I was

39
00:03:56.617 --> 00:04:01.775
wondering where his money came from when he interrupted my thoughts. "You're examining

40
00:04:01.776 --> 00:04:05.933
my shells and pearls, Professor?" he said. "They must

41
00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:14.259
be of interest to a naturalist like yourself, but they have a special significance to me because I collected them all myself." "No museum

42
00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:18.489
in Europe possesses such a collection," I said. "But if

43
00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:23.759
I exhaust all my admiration on it, I won't have any left for the Nautilus itself. I don't

44
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.291
wish to pry into your secrets, but I confess I'd like to know more about the submarine. I see

45
00:04:29.292 --> 00:04:33.584
instruments hanging on these walls that I'm not familiar with." "You'll find

46
00:04:33.585 --> 00:04:39.735
these same instruments in my own cabin, where I'll explain them to you," said the captain. "But first

47
00:04:39.736 --> 00:04:42.648
come look at your own cabin." He led

48
00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:50.640
me out of the gallery, through a passage, and into my cabin, which was really too elegant to be called a cabin. It was

49
00:04:50.641 --> 00:04:58.227
furnished with a bed, table, and several other pieces of furniture. I found myself thanking my host. "Your room

50
00:04:58.228 --> 00:05:03.473
adjoins mine, which opens into the gallery," Captain Nemo explained. We entered

51
00:05:03.474 --> 00:05:09.044
the captain's room, which had a severe, almost monk-like appearance to it. There was

52
00:05:09.045 --> 00:05:15.052
only a small bed and a table—no comforts, only the strictest necessities. Captain Nemo

53
00:05:15.053 --> 00:05:18.306
pointed to a seat and I sat down. He explained

54
00:05:18.307 --> 00:05:26.449
that here and in the gallery, he had all the instruments that he needed to navigate the sub and keep track of the weather. "But how

55
00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.203
do you power everything?" I asked. "Ah," said

56
00:05:30.204 --> 00:05:33.195
Captain Nemo. "There is

57
00:05:33.196 --> 00:05:43.259
something that reigns supreme onboard my vessel: this agent lights the submarine, warms it, and is the soul of my mechanical apparatus. This agent

58
00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.259
is electricity." "Electricity!" I

59
00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.112
cried in surprise. On land

60
00:05:49.113 --> 00:05:58.251
electricity was able to produce only limited amounts of power; it wasn't capable of powering such a fast and complicated vessel as the Nautilus . "But

61
00:05:58.252 --> 00:06:05.513
how are you able to do this? The batteries that you use to generate this marvelous force must be depleted very quickly." "That's

62
00:06:05.514 --> 00:06:11.926
true, but my batteries are powered by sodium and someday I'll show you how we mine that." "Do

63
00:06:11.927 --> 00:06:15.578
the batteries produce fresh air for the sub?" I asked. "We

64
00:06:15.579 --> 00:06:24.157
could do that and we have some reservoirs filled with air, but I prefer to rise to the surface every day to replenish our air supply." "Aha!"

65
00:06:24.158 --> 00:06:30.745
I said, for now I understood the sounds we'd heard onboard the Abraham Lincoln .

66
00:06:30.746 --> 00:06:36.316
The Nautilus really did rise to the surface of the ocean to breathe like a whale.

67
00:06:36.317 --> 00:06:40.896
We continued our tour and the captain showed me the engine room and the kitchen.

68
00:06:40.897 --> 00:06:46.150
The door to the crew's quarters was closed, so I couldn't tell how many men were onboard.

69
00:06:46.151 --> 00:06:51.801
He also showed me a small boat that could be released from the sub for fishing or pleasure.

70
00:06:51.802 --> 00:06:54.364
But I was still filled with questions.

71
00:06:54.365 --> 00:07:03.864
"How does the sub descend to great depths?" I asked. "How does it rise back to the surface? I hope I'm not asking too many questions."

72
00:07:03.865 --> 00:07:11.213
"I don't mind answering," replied Captain Nemo. He stared at me for a moment before adding a grim reminder.

73
00:07:11.214 --> 00:07:18.190
"Because you'll never be able to leave the Nautilus to tell anyone what you've seen."

